WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.447
A finance query sums the amounts of a million payments.

2
00:00:06.447 --> 00:00:10.671
In a row store, each page holds whole rows, one after another.

3
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:20.388
The amounts are spread through every page, so the scan reads all of them: 8,404 pages, 66 megabytes, for one field.

4
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:23.324
A column store turns the table on its side.

5
00:00:23.324 --> 00:00:29.229
Each field gets its own array, in row order, so row three is position three in every array.

6
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.609
Now the sum reads one array: a million amounts, 8 megabytes.

7
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.319
And neighbours in a column look alike.

8
00:00:36.319 --> 00:00:41.356
Here are twelve currencies: four different strings, stored in full, 84 bytes.

9
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:47.641
Dictionary encoding stores each string once, and replaces every value by its number in the list.

10
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:51.825
The numbers only go up to three, so two bits hold each one.

11
00:00:51.825 --> 00:00:54.801
The whole chunk shrinks from 84 bytes to 31.

12
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.484
When equal values sit together, run-length encoding does even better.

13
00:00:59.484 --> 00:01:02.864
Sorted by currency, the twelve values are four runs.

14
00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:09.945
No encoding wins everywhere, so the engine tries several on each chunk and keeps the smallest.

15
00:01:09.945 --> 00:01:13.384
DuckDB stores the whole table in 5.5 megabytes.

16
00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:15.221
Skipping is cheaper still.

17
00:01:15.221 --> 00:01:19.803
When the engine writes a chunk, it notes the smallest and largest value.

18
00:01:19.803 --> 00:01:21.331
That pair is a zone map.

19
00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:23.573
A query wants October fifteenth.

20
00:01:23.573 --> 00:01:28.672
Five chunks can't hold that day, so the scan skips them unread, and decodes one.

21
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:32.089
That only works because payments arrive in time order.

22
00:01:32.089 --> 00:01:36.624
Shuffle the rows, and every chunk spans the whole month: nothing is skipped.

23
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.948
A column store reads one field, compressed, and only from the chunks that can match.
